# compiler-rt, built for the musl target.
#
# This is what makes the CFI claim true rather than aspirational. clang's CFI
# emits calls into a runtime -- __cfi_slowpath, and the diagnostic handlers
# that turn a violation into a named report rather than SIGILL -- and that
# runtime ships as libclang_rt.cfi*.a, compiled for a specific target triple.
# The host's clang package carries one built for glibc. Nothing links it into
# an x86_64-linux-musl binary, so without this recipe every CFI-enabled link
# in the tree fails on undefined runtime symbols, and the obvious "fix" is to
# drop -fsanitize=cfi and never notice what was lost.
#
# Version-locked to the host clang deliberately: compiler-rt and the compiler
# that emits calls into it are one implementation split across two tarballs,
# and a mismatched pair fails at link time on a good day.
name: compiler-rt
version:
- '18'
- '1'
- '8'
dependencies: []
steps:

- stage: Prepare
  name: fetch
  dl_urls:
    @URL_COMPILER_RT@: '@SHA256_COMPILER_RT@'
    @URL_LLVM_CMAKE@: '@SHA256_LLVM_CMAKE@'
  run:
  - mkdir -p /build/src/compiler-rt /build/src/llvm-cmake /build/b/compiler-rt
  - tar -xf @DL_COMPILER_RT@ --no-same-owner -C /build/src/compiler-rt --strip-components=1
  # The second tarball is eight kilobytes of cmake modules shared by every
  # LLVM sub-project. compiler-rt's own cmake/ carries none of them, and
  # base-config-ix.cmake includes two -- ExtendPath and GetClangResourceDir --
  # on its first pass, so without this the configure step dies before it looks
  # at a single option.
  - tar -xf @DL_LLVM_CMAKE@ --no-same-owner -C /build/src/llvm-cmake --strip-components=1

# One patch, and it is the difference between this recipe compiling and not.
# sanitizer_common includes <linux/scc.h> unconditionally on Linux, and Linux
# 7.2 -- the LINUX pin in manifest/sources.lock, which linux-headers puts in
# the sysroot -- removed that header along with the AX.25 SCC drivers. Spelling
# that pin as a token here would be worse than wrong: tools/gates/versions.py
# reads a template's @URL_...@ tokens as its source list, so a mention in a
# comment would make this a three-source recipe whose sources disagree, and the
# gate would go quiet about it. Applied with
# no fuzz, so the day upstream guards the include themselves this fails here
# rather than applying somewhere it was not meant to.
- stage: Build
  name: patch
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - /bin/sh @RECIPE@/apply-patches.sh @RECIPE@/files/compiler-rt/patches /build/src/compiler-rt

# COMPILER_RT_BUILD_* is mostly off: this is not a sanitizer distribution, it
# is the handful of objects CFI and the stack protector need. Building the
# whole of asan/tsan/msan for a target we do not test them on would multiply
# the build time and ship libraries nothing links.
#
# COMPILER_RT_BUILD_SANITIZERS stays ON because lib/cfi lives under it, and
# COMPILER_RT_SANITIZERS_TO_BUILD is what makes that ON mean what the
# paragraph above claims. Left at its default of `all` it builds every
# sanitizer there is, and two of them do not survive this sysroot: gwp_asan
# includes <execinfo.h>, which musl does not have, and tsan's shared library
# wants -lstdc++, -lgcc_s and crtbeginS.o from a sysroot that at this point
# holds a freshly built musl and nothing else. `cfi` alone still brings
# sanitizer_common, interception, stats, lsan and ubsan, which lib/cfi needs
# and which lib/CMakeLists.txt adds whenever sanitizers are on at all; the
# result is libclang_rt.cfi, cfi_diag, ubsan_standalone and their supports.
#
# COMPILER_RT_HAS_VERSION_SCRIPT is a detected capability, and forcing it off
# says something untrue about the linker, which does support version scripts.
# It is here because it is the only switch compiler-rt offers over the thing
# that actually matters: it is the `if` around every SHARED runtime, and a
# shared runtime is what fails the link above. Nothing here loads a sanitizer
# runtime dynamically -- cross-DSO CFI links libclang_rt.cfi_diag.a into each
# DSO, statically, which is the whole point of building it here -- so the
# libraries this turns off are ones nothing would have opened. Measured: with
# these two set, the generated build has no shared target left in it at all.
#
# COMPILER_RT_STANDALONE_BUILD is what lets this be built without the rest of
# the LLVM tree present -- but "the rest of the tree" does not include the
# shared cmake modules, which a standalone build still reads and which the
# compiler-rt tarball does not ship.
#
# LLVM_COMMON_CMAKE_UTILS is the variable that names where they are, and it is
# the right one rather than CMAKE_MODULE_PATH: compiler-rt's CMakeLists.txt
# uses it twice, and only one of those two uses is a module lookup that
# CMAKE_MODULE_PATH could answer. Line 11 includes
# ${LLVM_COMMON_CMAKE_UTILS}/Modules/CMakePolicy.cmake by full path, before
# project() has even run; line 22 then splices ${LLVM_COMMON_CMAKE_UTILS} and
# its Modules/ into CMAKE_MODULE_PATH itself, which is what makes
# base-config-ix's `include(ExtendPath)` and `include(GetClangResourceDir)`
# resolve. Setting CMAKE_MODULE_PATH alone fixes the second and leaves the
# first failing, and since cmake accumulates configure errors rather than
# stopping at the first, the surviving error names base-config-ix and looks
# like the whole problem. Measured against the real tarballs: module path
# alone still dies at CMakeLists.txt:11.
#
# Nothing else is needed from LLVM's build files. compiler-rt finds the
# installed LLVM cmake package by itself -- `find_package(LLVM)` with no hint,
# which resolves through pm's read-only mirror of the host /usr, the same
# place @CC@ comes from. LLVM_CMAKE_DIR is deliberately not spelled out here:
# a literal /usr/lib/llvm-N/lib/cmake/llvm would be this container's layout
# baked into a recipe, and discovery already adapts. What is needed is that
# the package be installed at all, which is the Containerfile's job and is
# recorded in docs/host-requirements.md; without it compiler-rt falls back to
# CompilerRTMockLLVMCMakeConfig, which wants AddLLVM.cmake from the LLVM
# *source* tree and hard-errors when it is absent.
#
# Tombstone: -DLLVM_CONFIG_PATH= used to be on this line. It is upstream's
# deprecated spelling of LLVM_CMAKE_DIR, it was empty, and an empty value
# selects nothing; it is not coming back, because the supported knob is
# LLVM_CMAKE_DIR and the supported answer here is to let discovery run.
#
# CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX is not /usr, and that is the whole of what makes these
# runtimes reachable. clang resolves a sanitizer runtime, its own crt objects
# and the CFI ignorelist out of its RESOURCE DIRECTORY, and consults neither
# --sysroot nor -L nor -B on the way; manifest/toolchain.yaml therefore points
# -resource-dir at this prefix inside the sysroot, and this is the recipe that
# has to fill it. Under /usr the files landed where nothing looked, and every
# CFI-built package in the tree linked the build host's glibc copy instead.
#
# COMPILER_RT_BUILD_CRT is on for the same reason and not for its own: with
# --rtlib=compiler-rt clang wants clang_rt.crtbegin/crtend out of the resource
# directory, and falls back to gcc's crtbeginS.o when they are absent -- which
# on a host with no gcc at all is a bare filename lld cannot open.
#
# CMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_TARGET_TYPE=STATIC_LIBRARY is the one that makes this
# configure at all, and it is a bootstrap problem rather than a preference.
# cmake's first act on `project(... LANGUAGES C)` is to compile and LINK a
# trivial executable, and linking one against a sysroot holding nothing but a
# freshly built musl needs a runtime library that is not there yet:
#
#     /usr/bin/ld: cannot find libgcc_s.so.1: No such file or directory
#     clang: error: linker command failed with exit code 1
#
# clang reaches for libgcc_s because the alternative is compiler-rt, which is
# what this recipe exists to produce. STATIC_LIBRARY makes the check compile
# without linking, which is what every toolchain bootstrap does here and what
# LLVM's own documentation recommends for exactly this case.
- stage: Build
  name: configure
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake -S /build/src/compiler-rt -B /build/b/compiler-rt -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=None -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=@CC@ -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=@CXX@ -DCMAKE_AR=@AR@ -DCMAKE_RANLIB=@RANLIB@ -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER_TARGET=@ARCH_TRIPLE@ -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_TARGET=@ARCH_TRIPLE@ -DCMAKE_SYSROOT=/build/sysroot -DLLVM_COMMON_CMAKE_UTILS=/build/src/llvm-cmake -DCMAKE_TRY_COMPILE_TARGET_TYPE=STATIC_LIBRARY -DCOMPILER_RT_STANDALONE_BUILD=ON -DCOMPILER_RT_DEFAULT_TARGET_ONLY=ON -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_SANITIZERS=ON -DCOMPILER_RT_SANITIZERS_TO_BUILD=cfi -DCOMPILER_RT_HAS_VERSION_SCRIPT=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_BUILTINS=ON -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_CRT=ON -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_XRAY=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_LIBFUZZER=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_PROFILE=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_MEMPROF=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_BUILD_ORC=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_USE_LIBCXX=OFF -DCOMPILER_RT_CAN_EXECUTE_TESTS=OFF

- stage: Build
  name: compile
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake --build /build/b/compiler-rt -j @JOBS@

# The sysroot install goes through make rather than `cmake --install --prefix`.
# DESTDIR=/dest is in the environment of every step, and cmake prepends it to
# whatever --prefix says: that spelling wrote the whole tree into
# /dest/build/sysroot/usr and left the sysroot empty, so a sibling in the same
# bundle linked against nothing. A variable set on make's command line is
# exported, which is the DESTDIR cmake_install.cmake actually reads.
- stage: Build
  name: install
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - cmake --install /build/b/compiler-rt
  - make -C /build/b/compiler-rt install DESTDIR=/build/sysroot

# The fourth thing a resource directory has to carry, and the one compiler-rt
# does not build: clang's builtin headers. stddef.h, stdarg.h, limits.h and the
# per-architecture intrinsics belong to the compiler, not to its runtime
# library, so -resource-dir moves them along with everything else and a
# directory without them fails at the first #include <stddef.h>.
#
# The source is asked for rather than spelled -- tools/configure runs
# `clang -print-resource-dir` and substitutes the answer -- because a literal
# /usr/lib/llvm-N/lib/clang/N would be this container's layout baked into a
# recipe, which the discovery argument on the configure step above rejects for
# the cmake package too.
#
# `cp -R` rather than `cp -a`: -a preserves ownership, and pm's jail is a user
# namespace mapping only uid 0, so copying a file recorded under any other uid
# fails the same way a tarball without --no-same-owner does. Mode and
# timestamps are not load-bearing for a header.
- stage: Build
  name: stage-clang-headers
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - mkdir -p /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/include /build/sysroot@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/include
  - cp -R @CLANG_RESOURCE_DIR@/include/. /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/include/
  - cp -R @CLANG_RESOURCE_DIR@/include/. /build/sysroot@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/include/

# The three archives the rest of the tree is built on, named rather than
# counted: an empty directory would satisfy a `test -d` and leave the CFI flags
# in every later layer a lie that only shows up at the first link.
#
# cfi is the one that is load-bearing rather than illustrative: with the
# manifest's flag set -- cross-DSO, trapping -- it is the single runtime clang
# names on the link line, measured by asking the driver for its commands and
# reading back what it asked for. cfi_diag is checked beside it because it is
# what `cfi.trap: false` would name instead, and the manifest says that flip is
# temporary; a build that produced only one of the pair would pass this step
# today and fail at the first link on the day the flip is reversed.
#
# lib/linux with an -@ARCH@ suffix is compiler-rt's own default layout, not a
# choice made here: without LLVM_ENABLE_PER_TARGET_RUNTIME_DIR the install path
# is lib/${COMPILER_RT_OS_DIR} and every archive carries the target arch in its
# name. Tombstone: this step used to be `test -d /dest/usr/lib/@ARCH_TRIPLE@`,
# which is the per-target layout. That layout is not coming back by switching
# the option on: standalone, per-target installs flat into lib/ -- the triple
# component comes from COMPILER_RT_INSTALL_PATH, which a runtimes build sets
# and a standalone build does not -- so the option would buy a different wrong
# path rather than the one the check named.
#
# The last four lines are the resource directory's other contents, and they are
# checked here because each one fails somewhere else entirely when it is
# missing: no crtbegin and clang silently reaches for gcc's, no ignorelist and
# -fsanitize=cfi refuses to run at all, no stddef.h and every package in every
# later layer fails at its first include. This step is the only place that can
# see all four at once.
- stage: Test
  name: staged
  dl_urls: null
  run:
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/lib/linux/libclang_rt.cfi-@ARCH@.a
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/lib/linux/libclang_rt.cfi_diag-@ARCH@.a
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/lib/linux/libclang_rt.builtins-@ARCH@.a
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/lib/linux/clang_rt.crtbegin-@ARCH@.o
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/lib/linux/clang_rt.crtend-@ARCH@.o
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/share/cfi_ignorelist.txt
  - test -f /dest@RESOURCE_DIR_PREFIX@/include/stddef.h
